<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liber Abaci</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1202</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Liber Abaci (the Book of Calculation) is a treatise on arithmetic completed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fibonacci">Leonardo of Pisa</a>, known as Fibonacci, in 1202 and revised in 1228. It taught the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>–Arabic numerals and their use in commercial arithmetic, such as bookkeeping, currency conversion and the calculation of interest. It helped replace Roman numerals in European commerce and contains the rabbit problem that gives rise to the Fibonacci sequence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liber-abaci.jpg" alt="Liber Abaci" width="320" height="489" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liber_abbaci_magliab_f124r.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Liber Abaci, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> for the Book of Calculation, was written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fibonacci">Leonardo of Pisa</a> in 1202. No copies of that first version are known; a revised version dedicated to Michael Scot appeared in 1228. Leonardo wrote that his father was a public official at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Bugia</a> customshouse established for Pisan merchants, and that there he learned the art of the nine Indian figures. He later travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and Provence to study their methods, and he worked the book up in fifteen chapters.</p><p>Despite the title, the book is not about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abacus">abacus</a>: the word then meant calculation of any kind. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a> drew on Muslim scholars; of some 90 algebraic problems examined, 22 were found to come from a book on algebra written around 830 CE and 53 from Abu Kamil's book, written around 912 CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The first section introduces the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-arabic-numeral-system">Hindu–Arabic numeral system</a>, its arithmetic and methods of converting between systems, and gives the first known description of trial division. The second gives examples from commerce, such as currency and measure conversions and calculations of profit and interest. The third treats mathematical problems, including the Chinese remainder theorem, perfect numbers, Mersenne primes and a problem about the growth of a population of rabbits whose solution produces the sequence now named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fibonacci">Fibonacci</a>, though it is older. The fourth approximates irrational numbers such as square roots. The book also has proofs in Euclidean geometry and a notation for fractions that stands between Egyptian fractions and the vulgar fractions of today.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The book argued for the digits 0 to 9 and for place value at a time when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> used Roman numerals. For centuries the algorismists, followers of its style of calculation, remained in conflict with the abacists, who kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abacus">abacus</a> and Roman numerals. The spread of the new numerals was long drawn out, was not complete until the later part of the 16th century, and quickened in the 1500s with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>. At least nineteen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> containing parts of the 1228 text survive. There was no printed edition until Boncompagni's in 1857, and the first complete English translation was Sigler's, published in 2002.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Liber Abaci” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Leonardo Fibonacci’s <b>Liber Abaci</b> (1202) taught merchants Hindu-Arabic arithmetic, Italian merchants developed double-entry bookkeeping, set out in print by Luca Pacioli in 1494, and Florentine humanists such as Leonardo Bruni praised their republic’s liberty as the source of its greatness, an outlook the historian Hans Baron called civic…</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>The Indian numerals that al-Khwarizmi had explained reached Europe as “Arabic numerals”, commended to merchants by Leonardo of Pisa in his <b>Liber Abaci</b> of 1202.</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Its merchants brought back Arabic learning: Leonardo Fibonacci's <b>Liber Abaci</b> of 1202 introduced Hindu-Arabic numerals to Europe.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-pisa">Republic of Pisa</a></li></ul></section>
